<title>INDONESIA: Facts and figures on Indonesian election.</title>
<headline>Facts and figures on Indonesian election.</headline>
<dateline>JAKARTA 1997-04-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>Following are facts and figures on the Indonesian national and local elections:</p>
<p>- Campaign period April 27 to May 23.</p>
<p>- Voting May 29.</p>
<p>- Three political parties legally permitted to take part:</p>
<p>ruling Golkar party, which won 68 percent in last</p>
<p>elections in 1992, Moslem-oriented United Development</p>
<p>Party (PPP) which won 17 percent, and</p>
<p>Christian-Nationalist Indonesian Democratic Party (PDI)</p>
<p>which won 15 percent.</p>
<p>- Candidates: Golkar 825, PDI 744, PPP 716.</p>
<p>- Electorate: 124.7 million of population of 200 million.</p>
<p>- Minimum voting age 17, younger if married.</p>
<p>- Seats in House of Representatives: 500, of which 425</p>
<p>elected and 75 alloted to the military which does not</p>
<p>vote.</p>
<p>- Members elected by proportional representation.</p>
</text>
